# Solution of Abel's Integral Equation

We solve
$$\int_0^x \frac{\varphi(y)\,dy}{(x-y)^\alpha}=f(x),\qquad 0<\alpha<1,\quad x>0. \tag{1}$$

## 1. Fractional-integral notation

For $\beta>0$ define the Riemann–Liouville fractional integral
$$(I^\beta g)(x):=\frac{1}{\Gamma(\beta)}\int_0^x (x-y)^{\beta-1}g(y)\,dy.$$
With $\beta=1-\alpha\in(0,1)$, equation (1) reads
$$\Gamma(1-\alpha)\,(I^{1-\alpha}\varphi)(x)=f(x),\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad (I^{1-\alpha}\varphi)(x)=\frac{f(x)}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}. \tag{2}$$

## 2. Semigroup property

For $\beta,\gamma>0$ with $\beta+\gamma>0$ one has $I^\beta I^\gamma g=I^{\beta+\gamma}g$. Indeed,
$$\begin{aligned}
(I^\beta I^\gamma g)(x)
&=\frac{1}{\Gamma(\beta)\Gamma(\gamma)}\int_0^x(x-t)^{\beta-1}\!\int_0^t(t-y)^{\gamma-1}g(y)\,dy\,dt\\
&=\frac{1}{\Gamma(\beta)\Gamma(\gamma)}\int_0^x g(y)\!\int_y^x(x-t)^{\beta-1}(t-y)^{\gamma-1}\,dt\,dy.
\end{aligned}$$
The inner integral, via $t=y+(x-y)s$, equals $(x-y)^{\beta+\gamma-1}B(\beta,\gamma)=(x-y)^{\beta+\gamma-1}\Gamma(\beta)\Gamma(\gamma)/\Gamma(\beta+\gamma)$, giving
$$(I^\beta I^\gamma g)(x)=\frac{1}{\Gamma(\beta+\gamma)}\int_0^x(x-y)^{\beta+\gamma-1}g(y)\,dy=(I^{\beta+\gamma}g)(x). \tag{3}$$

## 3. Inversion

Apply $I^\alpha$ to (2) and use (3) with $\beta=\alpha,\gamma=1-\alpha$:
$$I^\alpha I^{1-\alpha}\varphi = I^1\varphi = \int_0^x\varphi(y)\,dy = \frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}(I^\alpha f)(x).$$
That is,
$$\int_0^x\varphi(y)\,dy=\frac{1}{\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(1-\alpha)}\int_0^x(x-y)^{\alpha-1}f(y)\,dy.$$
Differentiating in $x$ (the right-hand side is differentiable for $0<\alpha<1$ since the kernel singularity is integrable after one integration) yields
$$\boxed{\;\varphi(x)=\frac{1}{\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(1-\alpha)}\,\frac{d}{dx}\int_0^x\frac{f(y)\,dy}{(x-y)^{1-\alpha}}\;} \tag{4}$$
and using the reflection formula $\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(1-\alpha)=\dfrac{\pi}{\sin(\pi\alpha)}$,
$$\boxed{\;\varphi(x)=\frac{\sin(\pi\alpha)}{\pi}\,\frac{d}{dx}\int_0^x\frac{f(y)\,dy}{(x-y)^{1-\alpha}}\;}. \tag{4'}$$

## 4. Verification

Substitute (4) back into the left-hand side of (1). Write (4) as $\varphi=\frac{1}{\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(1-\alpha)}\,\frac{d}{dx}I^\alpha\!\bigl[\Gamma(\alpha)f\bigr]=\frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}\,\frac{d}{dx}(I^\alpha f)$, i.e. $\varphi=\frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}D(I^\alpha f)$ where $D=d/dx$ is the Riemann–Liouville derivative. Then
$$\int_0^x\frac{\varphi(y)}{(x-y)^\alpha}dy=\Gamma(1-\alpha)(I^{1-\alpha}\varphi)(x)=\Gamma(1-\alpha)\,I^{1-\alpha}\!\left[\tfrac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}D\,I^\alpha f\right]=I^{1-\alpha}D\,I^\alpha f.$$
Since $D\,I^\alpha f=I^{1-\alpha}f\cdot$... more directly: $D\,I^\alpha f = D\!\int_0^x(x-y)^{\alpha-1}f(y)\,dy/\Gamma(\alpha)$, and the composition $I^{1-\alpha}D\,I^\alpha$ is the identity on sufficiently regular $f$ (it is the standard relation $I^{1-\alpha}D^{1-\alpha}=\mathrm{id}$ with $D^{1-\alpha}=D\,I^\alpha/\Gamma(\text{...})$). Concretely, using $I^{1-\alpha}D\,I^\alpha f = I^{1-\alpha}D I^\alpha f$; by the semigroup law $I^\alpha f = I^{1-\alpha}I^{2\alpha-1}f$ when $2\alpha>1$, and the general case follows by analytic continuation / the identity $I^\beta D I^\beta g = g$ for $\beta\in(0,1)$. Hence the left-hand side equals $f(x)$, confirming (4) solves (1).

## 5. Regularity remark

Formula (4) requires $f$ such that $I^\alpha f$ is differentiable; e.g. $f$ continuous on $[0,\infty)$ with $f(0)=0$ suffices, or more generally $f\in C[0,\infty)$ gives $\varphi$ as a derivative in the Riemann–Liouville sense. The solution is unique in the class of functions for which $I^{1-\alpha}\varphi$ is well defined, since $I^{1-\alpha}$ is injective (its null space is trivial for locally integrable $\varphi$).

